Sure! To extract all key‑value pairs that come after the key `"userId"` in your `dataAnalysisContext` object, you can use the `getNextKeyValues` helper.  
First, make sure you have the context object available in your environment. Then you can call the function like this:

```json
{
  "name": "getNextKeyValues",
  "arguments": {
    "ctx": "<stringified dataAnalysisContext>",
    "currentKey": "userId"
  }
}
```

Replace `<stringified dataAnalysisContext>` with the JSON string of your actual context object. For example, if your context looks like this:

```js
const dataAnalysisContext = {
  userId: 12345,
  name: "Alice",
  age: 30,
  address: {
    city: "Wonderland",
    zip: "12345"
  },
  hobbies: ["reading", "coding"]
};
```

You would call the function as:

```json
{
  "name": "getNextKeyValues",
  "arguments": {
    "ctx": "{\"userId\":12345,\"name\":\"Alice\",\"age\":30,\"address\":{\"city\":\"Wonderland\",\"zip\":\"12345\"},\"hobbies\":[\"reading\",\"coding\"]}",
    "currentKey": "userId"
  }
}
```

This will return all key‑value pairs that follow `"userId"` up to the next nested object or array. If you need help formatting the string or have a different context structure, just let me know!
